course: biology
lesson: 118
title: Tom and Jerry: do real cats really chase mice?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In every episode of Tom and Jerry a grey cat crashes round a big kitchen after a cheeky mouse, gets flattened by an iron, pops back into shape and loses again. It is very funny, and almost none of it is how real cats and mice live.
explain: Real cats do hunt mice, and people have kept cats for thousands of years partly for that job, but a real hunting cat is silent: it creeps, waits and pounces, because a mouse runs at the first strange sound.
explain: A real mouse is tiny, about 20 grams, as heavy as four sugar cubes, runs on four legs, lives in an untidy nest of fluff inside the wall, and would choose peanut butter or chocolate over a holey wedge of cheese.
explain: And Tom's saucer of milk? Most grown-up cats cannot digest milk well, and it can upset their tummies, so a real cat needs a bowl of water instead.
fact: Real cats do hunt mice, and the hunting urge does not switch off when they are full, but many well-fed pet cats hunt only now and then and some never catch a mouse.
fact: A cat was buried next to a person in Cyprus about 9,500 years ago, so cats and people were already living together near stores of grain.
fact: Real mice prefer seeds, grain, nuts and sweet foods such as chocolate or peanut butter; they will nibble cheese, but it is not their favourite, and pest catchers bait traps with peanut butter.
fact: Real house mice build untidy nests inside walls, under floors, in lofts and behind cupboards, out of shredded paper, cloth, insulation and fluff, and can squeeze through a gap about as wide as a pencil.
fact: A house mouse weighs about 20 grams and an adult cat about 4 kilograms, about 200 times more; mice run on all four legs and only stand up to look or sniff.
fact: A mouse's heart beats about 500 to 600 times a minute, a cat's about 150 and yours about 80.
fact: A real cat would be badly hurt by a small part of what happens to Tom; animators squash and stretch bodies because it looks lively and funny, a rule they learn by drawing a bouncing ball.
fact: Most adult cats cannot digest milk sugar well, because they lose most of the body chemical that breaks it down after they stop feeding from their mother, so milk can give them diarrhoea; they need water.
fact: A cat usually wins if it really hunts a mouse, but mice escape very often, because they are fast and never go far from a hiding place; big brown rats fight back, and many city cats avoid them.
fact: Cats have only one life; the «nine lives» saying grew because cats are agile and good at twisting to land on their feet, and in some countries the saying is seven or six lives.
fact: Real hunting cats are almost silent: soft paw pads and claws pulled back inside the paws let them walk without clicking, unlike a dog.
fact: Many pet cats bring their catch home, perhaps because home is their safe den, and mother cats bring prey to teach their kittens, so the best thing is to stay calm and clean up, as shouting only scares the cat; a cat called Larry has the title of Chief Mouser at 10 Downing Street.
fact: Most pet cats are ordinary mixed cats, and grey, which is a «diluted» black, is common; almost all tortoiseshell and calico cats are female.
exercise: Why have people kept cats for thousands of years? | Partly to hunt the mice that ate their grain | To guard the house and bark at strangers like dogs | To pull small carts | To catch fish in the river for dinner
exercise: Where was a cat found buried next to a person about 9,500 years ago? | Cyprus | London | New York | Antarctica
exercise: Which food would a real mouse choose first? | Peanut butter or chocolate | A big wedge of yellow holey cheese | A raw onion | A slice of lemon
exercise: What do pest catchers usually put in mouse traps? | Peanut butter or chocolate | Cheese with big holes | Salt and pepper | Lettuce leaves
exercise: What is a real house mouse's home like? | An untidy nest of paper, cloth and fluff hidden in a wall | A neat arched door in the skirting board with a little bed inside | A hole dug in the middle of the carpet | A tiny wooden house on the shelf
exercise: How small a gap can a mouse squeeze through? | About as wide as a pencil | About as wide as a door | About as wide as a hair | About as wide as a football
exercise: How much does a house mouse weigh? | About 20 grams, like four sugar cubes | About 2 kilograms, like a bag of sugar | About as much as a cat | About 200 grams, like an apple
exercise: How do real mice usually move? | On all four legs, standing up only to look or sniff | Always on two legs like Jerry, even when running fast | By hopping like a kangaroo on long back legs | By sliding on their tummies
exercise: About how fast does a mouse's heart beat? | About 500 to 600 times a minute | About 8 times a minute | About 80 times a minute, like yours | About 5,000 times a minute
exercise: What would happen to a real cat flattened by an iron? | It would be badly hurt and need a vet | It would pop back into shape like Tom | It would grow taller | It would feel nothing at all
exercise: Why do animators squash and stretch cartoon bodies? | It makes movement look lively and funny | Real cat and mouse bodies work that way | Cats are made of rubber | To teach children real biology
exercise: What should a grown-up cat drink? | Water | A saucer of cow's milk | Lemonade | Coffee with sugar
exercise: Why can milk upset an adult cat's tummy? | Cats lose most of the chemical that breaks down milk sugar after kittenhood | Milk is too cold for cats | Cats are allergic to the colour white, so anything white makes them sick | Milk makes cats grow too fast, so their tummies stretch and hurt
exercise: Who usually wins when a real cat truly hunts a mouse? | Usually the cat, though mice often escape | Always the mouse, just like Jerry in the cartoon | Nobody, they become friends | Always the cat, every single time it tries
exercise: How many lives does a real cat have? | One | Nine | Seven | Three
exercise: Why does a cat walk silently on a hard floor? | Soft pads, and claws pulled back inside the paws | It walks on tiptoe with its claws stuck out | It floats slightly above the ground when it hunts | It wears fur slippers
exercise: Who is Larry? | A cat with the title of Chief Mouser at 10 Downing Street | Jerry's cousin in the cartoon | A famous mouse that lives in a London zoo | The big bulldog who guards the house in Tom and Jerry
exercise: Which cats are almost always female? | Tortoiseshell and calico cats | Grey and white cats | Black cats | Ginger cats
test: «Tom never catches Jerry so cats are rubbish at hunting innit» What is right? | Real cats are skilled hunters, though mice often escape | Yes, real cats never catch anything, they just sleep all day | Mice always beat cats in a fight | Cats only hunt cheese, never mice
test: «my cat's fat and lazy, he won't hunt anything, he's full» What is true? | The hunting urge does not switch off when full, so he may still chase things | A full cat can never hunt again | Fat cats hunt twice as much as thin ones, because they need more food to stay fat | Cats only hunt when the moon is full and the nights are bright enough to see
test: «Tom crashes about screaming after Jerry» What would happen to a real cat that hunted like that? | It would catch nothing, as mice run at the first noise | It would catch more mice than ever, as the noise freezes them | Mice would freeze and wait for it | It would scare the mice so much they ran into its mouth
test: «my nan gives her cat a saucer of milk every night, like Tom» What is the best advice? | Give water; most adult cats cannot digest milk well | Milk is the best drink for every cat, young or old | Add sugar so the cat likes it more | Give the cat cold coffee instead
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry is almost as big as Tom's paw. About how many times heavier is a real cat than a mouse? | About 200 times | About 2 times | About 20,000 times | About the same
test: «my cat brought a dead mouse to the door, gross, why??» What is a good idea about why? | Home is its safe den, and mother cats bring prey for kittens | It is paying rent for the food and the warm bed you give it | It wants you to cook it | It is trying to poison you with a mouse that it found
test: Your cat brings you a mouse. What is the best way to react? | Stay calm, move the cat away and clean up, since shouting only scares it | Shout at the cat until it learns | Reward it with a saucer of milk | Give the mouse back to the cat to eat
test: «Garfield hates mice and loves lasagne, real cats too?» What is right? | Real cats are meat-eaters that may chase mice; lasagne is not good cat food | Real cats live on pasta | Real cats are scared of all mice and run away when one squeaks | Real cats only eat vegetables, so they love the tomato in lasagne
test: Why might a city cat leave a big rat alone but chase a mouse? | A big rat can fight back hard and hurt the cat | Rats are too slow to be fun | Cats cannot see rats | Rats are always friends with cats
test: «Jerry, Mickey Mouse and Speedy Gonzales all walk on 2 legs, so 3 real mice would have how many legs?» | 12, four legs each | 6, two legs each | 9, three legs each | 3, one leg each
test: «Tom, Jerry and Spike the dog in one room, how many tails and ears altogether» | 9, three tails and six ears | 6, two tails and four ears | 12, four each | 3, one tail each and no ears
test: Why do you think people say cats have nine lives, not dogs? | Cats are agile and often walk away from falls that look bad | Cats are made of a stronger kind of bone than any other animal | Dogs have no lives at all | A scientist counted them
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dietary preferences of the house mouse? | It prefers seeds, grain, nuts and sweet foods, and consumes cheese only occasionally | It subsists principally upon cheese of the variety containing holes | It consumes exclusively animal flesh | It requires no nourishment whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which cow's milk is unsuitable for adult domestic cats. | Adult cats lack sufficient quantities of the enzyme that digests milk sugar | Milk is lethal to all mammals after weaning | Milk is excessively cold for feline consumption | Cats are incapable of drinking any liquid
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hunting conduct of the domestic cat. | It approaches its prey silently, with claws retracted, before pouncing | It pursues its prey noisily in order to induce panic | It hunts exclusively in the company of other cats of the same household | It abstains from hunting on every occasion once it has been adequately fed
exam: Ascertain the approximate ratio of the mass of an adult domestic cat to that of a house mouse. | Approximately two hundred to one | Approximately two to one | Approximately twenty thousand to one | Approximately one to one
